One assumption is not yet verified: that two HEAD writes of one directory never share a greatest `t`. H0 must prove it; if any HEAD move commits no transaction, the derived sequence fails and section 5.2 reopens.

## Questions for the owner

1. Does "always" reach into a live session? The design keeps a session on what it linked and needs `(reset)`; relink in place would need its own design.
2. What should a bare `q` see after `dht join`? Today it sees the publisher's facts through hydration. Here it sees only the reader's own index, and the publisher's is reached with `(dao.space.dht/q <manifest> ...)`.
3. May a publishing node re-serve the heads it follows by default? It keeps a head reachable while its publisher is offline, and it reveals whom that node follows.
4. Is every HEAD move the head you mean? A follower then loads a new index for each round the publisher evaluates, not only for each `yin.link/publish`.
